WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.137
This is the executive
briefing for Book 03 of the

3
00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:07.850
Federal Modernization
Workbook, HR-Driven Identity.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.433
It's for the people who make the calls:
the CIO, the Chief Human Capital

5
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:17.425
Officer and HR leadership, the CISO,
the privacy officer and the sponsors.

6
00:00:17.425 --> 00:00:21.548
Access is now decided by
identity, and the facts it reads,

7
00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:26.837
like organization, position, status
and supervisor, only HR originates.

8
00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:30.549
In most agencies those facts
reach the directory by ticket.

9
00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:34.955
And two migrations already under way
rebuild both ends of that connection,

10
00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:37.300
so this is the cheapest moment you'll get.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.537
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

12
00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:44.913
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:44.913 --> 00:00:47.990
First, where this book sits in the series.

14
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:57.004
Here's why this matters: the series
moves four populations to the cloud,

15
00:00:57.004 --> 00:01:00.453
each on its own track, and
Book 03 is the HR track.

16
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:01.577
It's about people.

17
00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:05.942
Employee accounts follow the HR
record; contractor accounts follow the

18
00:01:05.942 --> 00:01:10.089
contractor source of authority,
which is still the agency's decision.

19
00:01:10.089 --> 00:01:14.978
Joining, moving and leaving then
change access by rule, not by ticket.

20
00:01:14.978 --> 00:01:19.273
You'll know you're there when every
enabled account matches an authoritative

21
00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:23.166
record or an owned exception, and no
hand-kept group membership is left.

22
00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:26.302
Devices and servers are Book 05;

23
00:01:26.302 --> 00:01:31.490
service accounts and AI agents
are Books 02, 06 and 17.

24
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.766
And the design supports the
federal account-management

25
00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:37.902
and personnel controls; it
doesn't close them on its own.

26
00:01:37.902 --> 00:01:39.970
Now, what access runs on.

27
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.388
A sign-in token makes claims:
this person works here,

28
00:01:47.388 --> 00:01:50.445
holds this job, reports
to this supervisor.

29
00:01:50.445 --> 00:01:53.545
The identity platform can
prove who signed the claim.

30
00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.357
It can't prove the claim is true.

31
00:01:55.357 --> 00:01:58.187
That matters now, because under Zero Trust

32
00:01:58.187 --> 00:02:01.420
every request is evaluated
against those facts.

33
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.458
Conditional Access and dynamic
groups read organization,

34
00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.683
position, status and supervisor.

35
00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:12.094
So a wrong department isn't a typo
anymore; it's the wrong access.

36
00:02:12.094 --> 00:02:16.084
And a stale status is a working
token for someone who has left,

37
00:02:16.084 --> 00:02:17.520
usable from anywhere.

38
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.720
Federal guidance points to HR as the
authoritative source, without naming a product.

39
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.070
So the accuracy of employee data
is now the accuracy of access.

40
00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.500
So why do orphans keep coming back?

41
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.515
Ask who works here, what they
do and who they report to,

42
00:02:36.515 --> 00:02:38.338
and no single system answers.

43
00:02:38.338 --> 00:02:43.295
The answers are scattered across
regional organizational unit (OU) trees,

44
00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:47.276
application account stores,
spreadsheets and slide decks.

45
00:02:47.276 --> 00:02:51.613
HR records a separation two weeks
early, but nothing is listening.

46
00:02:51.613 --> 00:02:54.325
On the day, every account is still live.

47
00:02:54.325 --> 00:02:58.312
A ticket gets the main account days
later, but not the applications.

48
00:02:58.312 --> 00:03:02.238
Months on, a penetration test
signs in with a leftover account.

49
00:03:02.238 --> 00:03:03.375
Nobody failed.

50
00:03:03.375 --> 00:03:06.913
There's simply no channel from
HR to the systems that act.

51
00:03:06.913 --> 00:03:11.125
Cleaning up, or reviewing harder,
can't fix a missing channel.

52
00:03:11.125 --> 00:03:14.822
So the decision: designate HR
as the source of authority,

53
00:03:14.822 --> 00:03:17.313
and build it into both migrations now.

54
00:03:17.313 --> 00:03:18.500
Why now?

55
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.663
Federal HR 2.0 is coming regardless.

56
00:03:25.663 --> 00:03:30.501
One government-wide platform replaces
more than a hundred HR systems,

57
00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:35.174
Oracle won the award in June, and
the second wave moves in fiscal 2027.

58
00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:37.175
You don't choose whether to implement it.

59
00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:39.637
You choose whether identity rides along.

60
00:03:39.637 --> 00:03:41.865
And this is the cheapest moment,

61
00:03:41.865 --> 00:03:47.175
because HR 2.0 rebuilds the interconnections
and cleans the HR data anyway.

62
00:03:47.175 --> 00:03:50.700
But nothing in the program
makes HR drive your directory.

63
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.212
That's yours to propose.

64
00:03:52.212 --> 00:03:55.533
The window closes when your
interconnection goes live,

65
00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:57.987
around month five in the sample timeline.

66
00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:03.168
So in the first phase, name the identity
feed as one of your integration points,

67
00:04:03.168 --> 00:04:06.163
and name HR as the source
in your Entra ID plan.

68
00:04:06.163 --> 00:04:07.930
Next, the data.

69
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.337
The HR system is where four
facts are created: the worker,

70
00:04:15.337 --> 00:04:18.755
the position, the assignment
and the reporting line.

71
00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:21.705
HR doesn't compete with
the identity platform.

72
00:04:21.705 --> 00:04:24.341
HR owns who a person
is and where they sit;

73
00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:29.055
the identity platform owns how they
prove it and what they can reach now.

74
00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:32.797
HR also knows about hires and
separations before they happen,

75
00:04:32.797 --> 00:04:36.905
which is what makes day-one readiness
and same-day removal possible.

76
00:04:36.905 --> 00:04:40.505
The rule that holds it together:
corrections flow upstream.

77
00:04:40.505 --> 00:04:44.787
A wrong department is fixed in HR,
never by hand in the directory,

78
00:04:44.787 --> 00:04:48.080
because a hand edit is just
a new copy that drifts.

79
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.636
So the governance body signs one map:

80
00:04:50.636 --> 00:04:54.555
every attribute, its owner
and its quality expectation.

81
00:04:54.555 --> 00:04:56.210
Next, keys.

82
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.398
Every person needs one key the
agency controls and never changes.

83
00:05:04.398 --> 00:05:05.309
Here's why.

84
00:05:05.309 --> 00:05:09.535
It's tempting to use the HR system's
own person number as the key.

85
00:05:09.535 --> 00:05:13.143
But that number changes
when the HR system changes,

86
00:05:13.143 --> 00:05:15.635
and Core HCM is exactly that change.

87
00:05:15.635 --> 00:05:17.460
Contractors come from elsewhere.

88
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.719
So pick an enterprise key, often
an identifier you already have,

89
00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:23.848
and map every other number to it.

90
00:05:23.848 --> 00:05:28.260
Then the move to the new platform changes
one mapping, and nothing downstream.

91
00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.088
A returning employee is the same
person, not a new identity,

92
00:05:32.088 --> 00:05:35.198
and a contractor who
converts keeps one record.

93
00:05:35.198 --> 00:05:38.348
And the Social Security
number is never the join key.

94
00:05:38.348 --> 00:05:42.261
The governance body makes this
call before Phase 1 closes.

95
00:05:42.261 --> 00:05:44.230
Next, the standard.

96
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.155
The platform uses Oracle's field
names, but they aren't the contract.

97
00:05:52.155 --> 00:05:57.586
The durable contract is OPM's standard
personnel data: the same data elements

98
00:05:57.586 --> 00:06:02.593
and codes in every agency, and a
standard code on every personnel action.

99
00:06:02.593 --> 00:06:08.075
If you map identity to the standard once,
that one crosswalk covers every employee,

100
00:06:08.075 --> 00:06:13.317
survives the next vendor change, and can
be reused by any agency on the platform.

101
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:14.867
A few rules keep it clean.

102
00:06:14.867 --> 00:06:18.343
Use the attributes Entra
already understands first.

103
00:06:18.343 --> 00:06:23.580
Put codes in access rules, never display
names, because names get retyped.

104
00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.718
And remember the standard
covers federal employees only.

105
00:06:26.718 --> 00:06:31.049
The decision: the data architect
builds one governed crosswalk,

106
00:06:31.049 --> 00:06:33.255
and HR owns the code lists it uses.

107
00:06:33.255 --> 00:06:35.170
Now, the data flow.

108
00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.170
Here's the pipeline at leadership level.

109
00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:46.269
HR publishes worker records, a small
service translates them into one standard

110
00:06:46.269 --> 00:06:50.982
model, and Entra's provisioning service
creates and updates the accounts.

111
00:06:50.982 --> 00:06:54.373
Oracle's platform has no
ready-made Entra connector,

112
00:06:54.373 --> 00:06:59.183
so Microsoft's own guidance is this
pattern, with a service the agency runs.

113
00:06:59.183 --> 00:07:00.532
That service matters.

114
00:07:00.532 --> 00:07:05.326
It checks every record and refuses
bad ones, processes changes in order,

115
00:07:05.326 --> 00:07:09.795
and keeps a record of every event,
so every account has a paper trail.

116
00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.835
Accounts land in Active Directory for now,

117
00:07:12.835 --> 00:07:16.570
and directly in Entra ID as
each group of users moves.

118
00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.402
Because this service handles
personnel data inside your boundary,

119
00:07:20.402 --> 00:07:21.908
it needs an authorization.

120
00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:24.983
The authorizing official
grants it before the pilot.

121
00:07:24.983 --> 00:07:26.740
Next, the people.

122
00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:35.190
This is an organizational change
first, and most owners sit outside IT.

123
00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:40.627
Owners create the facts: HR owns personnel
data and its quality, OPM owns the

124
00:07:40.627 --> 00:07:46.590
platform and the standards, and contractor
sources own the people HR doesn't cover.

125
00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:51.305
Users consume the facts but never change
them: the identity team, security,

126
00:07:51.305 --> 00:07:53.702
application owners, and the help desk,

127
00:07:53.702 --> 00:07:57.953
which now routes data errors to
their owner instead of fixing them.

128
00:07:57.953 --> 00:08:00.665
Guards decide what can be used: privacy,

129
00:08:00.665 --> 00:08:03.802
the PIV office, and the
authorizing official.

130
00:08:03.802 --> 00:08:06.687
A data architect keeps it all consistent:

131
00:08:06.687 --> 00:08:09.827
one name, one meaning,
one owner per attribute.

132
00:08:09.827 --> 00:08:13.011
The decision for HR
leadership: name an owner,

133
00:08:13.011 --> 00:08:15.591
and a quality bar, for each attribute.

134
00:08:15.591 --> 00:08:17.350
Now, who decides.

135
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.425
One decider per row.

136
00:08:22.425 --> 00:08:27.005
The governance body takes the decisions
that bind several offices at once:

137
00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:30.625
the attribute map, the person
key and the contractor source.

138
00:08:30.625 --> 00:08:32.613
HR owns what its data means.

139
00:08:32.613 --> 00:08:34.699
The data architect, the model.

140
00:08:34.699 --> 00:08:37.213
Privacy owns the disclosure decision.

141
00:08:37.213 --> 00:08:39.713
Contracting owns contractor sponsorship.

142
00:08:39.713 --> 00:08:41.738
The PIV office owns the card.

143
00:08:41.738 --> 00:08:45.300
Application owners decide
access to their applications.

144
00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.588
And the authorizing official
accepts the remaining risk.

145
00:08:48.588 --> 00:08:52.732
The failures on the right share one
root: in a ticket-driven estate,

146
00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:55.801
whoever can make a change
ends up owning the data.

147
00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.287
That's how a leaver ends
up owned by nobody.

148
00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:01.118
So security owns the
leaver path end to end,

149
00:09:01.118 --> 00:09:05.162
and the authorizing official
weighs in on any looser timing.

150
00:09:05.162 --> 00:09:07.050
Then, two guards.

151
00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.200
Two guards bring clocks
you can't compress.

152
00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.756
First, privacy: moving
HR records into identity

153
00:09:16.756 --> 00:09:20.312
systems is a disclosure,
and needs a legal basis.

154
00:09:20.312 --> 00:09:24.496
If records leave the agency, a new
routine use has to be published,

155
00:09:24.496 --> 00:09:27.575
and it can't be relied on
for at least thirty days.

156
00:09:27.575 --> 00:09:30.491
The privacy impact
assessment needs updating,

157
00:09:30.491 --> 00:09:35.350
and the feed should carry only the
attributes access decisions actually read.

158
00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:38.662
That's the privacy office's
call, not the project's.

159
00:09:38.662 --> 00:09:40.250
Then the PIV office.

160
00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:43.029
Only the agency can
sponsor and approve a card,

161
00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:47.562
and a card that can't be collected must
be terminated within eighteen hours.

162
00:09:47.562 --> 00:09:51.212
In this model, the separation
record is the notification.

163
00:09:51.212 --> 00:09:53.500
So bring privacy in at Phase 1.

164
00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.190
Now, how it runs.

165
00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.157
The shift: nobody remembers
to disable a leaver;

166
00:10:02.157 --> 00:10:05.703
the separation record does
it, on the effective date.

167
00:10:05.703 --> 00:10:08.314
Every lifecycle event comes from HR.

168
00:10:08.314 --> 00:10:10.715
A new hire is ready before day one.

169
00:10:10.715 --> 00:10:14.840
A mover loses the old job's
access as the new job's arrives.

170
00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.615
A leaver is blocked first.

171
00:10:16.615 --> 00:10:21.515
A rehire comes back as the same person,
and a conversion keeps one identity.

172
00:10:21.515 --> 00:10:24.636
For federal employees, the
triggers already exist:

173
00:10:24.636 --> 00:10:29.690
every personnel action carries a standard
code that says what kind of action it is.

174
00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:31.728
A few rules prevent real harm.

175
00:10:31.728 --> 00:10:34.202
Leave without pay is not a separation.

176
00:10:34.202 --> 00:10:36.427
A separation wins any tie.

177
00:10:36.427 --> 00:10:40.091
And a cancelled separation
restores access, with evidence.

178
00:10:40.091 --> 00:10:43.452
HR validates those rules
before anything automates.

179
00:10:43.452 --> 00:10:45.250
Then, credentials.

180
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.981
The trap: disabling an
account doesn't revoke

181
00:10:51.981 --> 00:10:54.800
a PIV certificate or a device certificate.

182
00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.338
Both stay valid until
someone revokes them.

183
00:10:57.338 --> 00:11:00.341
So a lifecycle that stops at
the account leaves a former

184
00:11:00.341 --> 00:11:03.475
employee's credentials working
wherever they're trusted.

185
00:11:03.475 --> 00:11:05.400
The leaver sequence fixes that.

186
00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:10.363
Block sign-in and end sessions first,
which closes Entra immediately.

187
00:11:10.363 --> 00:11:13.801
Then revoke the card,
wipe or retire devices,

188
00:11:13.801 --> 00:11:17.525
and remove access, in
parallel, confirming each.

189
00:11:17.525 --> 00:11:19.138
Delete the account last.

190
00:11:19.138 --> 00:11:24.625
One more decision: the link between a PIV
card and its account decides who signs in.

191
00:11:24.625 --> 00:11:29.575
So only one automated system, the
card-management integration, may write it.

192
00:11:29.575 --> 00:11:34.000
Security sets that rule, and the
PIV office runs the card system.

193
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.110
Now, the open question about contractors.

194
00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.048
One decision is still open: where
contractor identities come from.

195
00:11:45.048 --> 00:11:49.247
The new HR platform covers federal
employees, not contractors.

196
00:11:49.247 --> 00:11:53.132
Today they usually live in spreadsheets
kept by contracting officers'

197
00:11:53.132 --> 00:11:56.686
representatives, and a spreadsheet
can't be a source of authority.

198
00:11:56.686 --> 00:12:01.931
Candidates include the new platform's
contingent worker record, the PIV sponsorship

199
00:12:01.931 --> 00:12:06.585
system, a contractor suitability
register, or a separate lifecycle service.

200
00:12:06.585 --> 00:12:08.323
None is complete on its own.

201
00:12:08.323 --> 00:12:10.323
So give each fact one authority,

202
00:12:10.323 --> 00:12:14.247
and make a federal sponsor
accountable for every contractor.

203
00:12:14.247 --> 00:12:18.364
Access then ends at the first
end condition: the contract ends,

204
00:12:18.364 --> 00:12:22.560
vetting is withdrawn, the card is
revoked, or the sponsor leaves.

205
00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.635
Start by reconciling
what exists, read-only.

206
00:12:25.635 --> 00:12:28.197
The governance body decides in Phase 1.

207
00:12:28.197 --> 00:12:29.610
Here's the plan.

208
00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.085
The plan has seven phases,
each gated on the one before.

209
00:12:37.085 --> 00:12:39.324
One caution runs through all of it:

210
00:12:39.324 --> 00:12:42.523
the source has to be correct,
not just designated.

211
00:12:42.523 --> 00:12:47.536
A bad HR feed produces bad access faster
than any ticket queue ever could.

212
00:12:47.536 --> 00:12:52.647
That's why the early phases fix data at the
source, though users see nothing change.

213
00:12:52.647 --> 00:12:57.027
The gate numbers, like ninety-nine
percent matching and zero missed leavers

214
00:12:57.027 --> 00:13:01.059
in a pay period, are proposed starting
points that the agency approves.

215
00:13:01.059 --> 00:13:05.735
The pilot runs in shadow mode first,
and automation starts with leavers.

216
00:13:05.735 --> 00:13:09.898
The wider program's foundation gate
requires the HR feed to be live,

217
00:13:09.898 --> 00:13:12.698
so a broken connector
can't stall the rollout.

218
00:13:12.698 --> 00:13:14.335
The steward holds that gate.

219
00:13:14.335 --> 00:13:16.090
Then, cutover.

220
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:25.165
Cutover happens one group of users at a
time, as each group moves to Entra ID.

221
00:13:25.165 --> 00:13:30.924
Before a group cuts over, acceptance tests
in plain business language are signed:

222
00:13:30.924 --> 00:13:33.330
a new hire, a transfer, a separation,

223
00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:36.940
leave without pay, a cancelled
action, a conversion.

224
00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:40.420
Anything that could leave a
separated person with access,

225
00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.102
or lock out someone
active, stops the cutover.

226
00:13:43.102 --> 00:13:46.774
Then there's a dry run on real
data with no changes written,

227
00:13:46.774 --> 00:13:48.414
and a rollback plan ready.

228
00:13:48.414 --> 00:13:52.553
For seven days afterward, every
change must trace back to HR.

229
00:13:52.553 --> 00:13:55.341
When that holds, the ticket path closes.

230
00:13:55.341 --> 00:13:58.415
Nobody requests an account
for a new hire anymore.

231
00:13:58.415 --> 00:14:02.165
HR, the ISSO and the business owner sign.

232
00:14:02.165 --> 00:14:03.940
How will you know it works?

233
00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:08.752
You can measure it.

234
00:14:08.752 --> 00:14:12.926
The book proposes six numbers: how
fast a separation blocks access,

235
00:14:12.926 --> 00:14:15.279
how fast every credential is revoked,

236
00:14:15.279 --> 00:14:18.390
how fast a transfer's
leftover access is reviewed,

237
00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:23.094
how many new hires are ready on day one,
how many accounts match a real worker,

238
00:14:23.094 --> 00:14:27.116
and how much access comes from
rules instead of hand assignment.

239
00:14:27.116 --> 00:14:30.253
None of those numbers exists
in a ticket-driven estate.

240
00:14:30.253 --> 00:14:33.016
Expect a spike of findings
in the first month.

241
00:14:33.016 --> 00:14:36.159
That's the measurement finally
seeing what was already there,

242
00:14:36.159 --> 00:14:37.453
not the pipeline failing.

243
00:14:37.453 --> 00:14:40.128
And keep the evidence
outside the directory,

244
00:14:40.128 --> 00:14:43.527
because Entra keeps most logs
for only about thirty days.

245
00:14:43.527 --> 00:14:48.853
The ISSO owns the evidence, and
security sets the leaver thresholds.

246
00:14:48.853 --> 00:14:51.090
Let's bring the decisions together.

247
00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.952
The decisions, in one place.

248
00:14:56.952 --> 00:15:01.877
The governance body designates HR as
the source now, signs the attribute map,

249
00:15:01.877 --> 00:15:05.628
chooses the person key and
settles the contractor source.

250
00:15:05.628 --> 00:15:10.766
Your HR 2.0 contact names the
identity feed in the first phase.

251
00:15:10.766 --> 00:15:16.127
HR leadership names attribute owners, and
the data architect builds the crosswalk.

252
00:15:16.127 --> 00:15:19.152
Privacy settles the
disclosure basis early.

253
00:15:19.152 --> 00:15:22.916
The authorizing official authorizes
the feed before the pilot.

254
00:15:22.916 --> 00:15:26.240
Security owns the leaver
path and the card binding.

255
00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.765
The steward holds the rollout
until the HR feed is live.

256
00:15:29.765 --> 00:15:33.590
And each population's ticket path
closes once the proof holds.

257
00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:36.315
Nobody requests an account for a new hire.

258
00:15:36.315 --> 00:15:39.377
The hire causes it, and
the separation ends it.

259
00:15:39.377 --> 00:15:41.627
Book 03 has the detail.

260
00:15:41.627 --> 00:15:42.450
Thank you.
